Z||000000||Joshua||11-09-2000||04:07 AM||||HELP!!!!!!<P>  I have a 1988 4 banger with this crazy distibutorless ignition (DIS) that is killin' me.<BR>  I sold the car to a friend with 126k miles on the clock an he returned it to me three months later on a tow truck.  It just won't start!  I put three sets of plugs in it, new wires, and for the outragous price of $197.00, a new igition module, and still nothing.  I'm getting fuel, I'm getting strong spark on plug four and it gets weaker untill almost nothing on plug one.  I wanted to check the fault codes, but the car came to me with a dead battery.  If this car had a normal cap and rotor I'd be done.  This is month number two of toying with it.  Parts cost "10", failure rating "10", frustration level, way past "100".  Please help me.  I can't just keep guessing at $200.00 a pop, and to add injury to insult, the licence plates and insurance came due two weeks ago, which I paid, on a car I can't drive!!!!  I've had this car since 1989 and it has served me well with no problems untill now.  Granted it can't be worth more than $1000.00, so I really can't justify spending a grand in shop repairs or parts, but right now it's a paper weight that I pay a parking fee on!  HELP!!!!<P><BR>               Desperate,<P>                   Joshua||||reg||5
Z||000001||BroCar 87||11-09-2000||05:07 AM||||Hey JOSHUA, I have an 87 and also had a problem simular to that. Did you replace the crank sensor? It is behind the ignition module. I had to replace everything on top of that before I found that problem. Also, there is a link at the bottom of this page called the Ogres cave. GO THERE!!! He is a big help with the 4 cylinder DIS ignitioned fieros. I see your fairly close to where I live. I live in Jacksonville. <P>OHHH sorry let me be the first to WELCOME you to the forum. This is a very informative site. Addictive too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I check up on this site maybe twice a day. And there are people worse than me about that too... right 2xsess!! lmao.<BR>So do you have any pics of your fiero that you can share with us. Tell us more about it and yourself.<BR>I'll be checking back with ya soon.<BR>Good luck with it!<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature2.jpg">  <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>   Custom dual side exit exhaust!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||11-09-2000||07:13 AM||||Joshua,<P>1st, Welcome to the Forum!<P>Yes, check your crank sensor. Check also both coil packs, if these items show to be good replace the brain box. I have an '88 2.5 that did the same thing to me several years ago and had the same headaches you have now. Brain box was the problem all along.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||11-09-2000||12:19 PM||||Those coil packs do have an occasional habit of cracking around the terminals or just plain going bad. I worked on a Corsica 3.1 that had only one cylinder with weak spark and it turned out to be the coil pack.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-09-2000||12:31 PM||||the most current version of the DIS article is at: <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>http://fierocave.shorturl.com</A>  read the whole article make sure the shield between the coils and module is not gone missing.<P>It's getting good spark that dies off. check the grounds bolted to the tranny near the ignition brick. the main brick ground is on that bunch I think.<P>Otherwise it sounds like coils. replace the coils as a set.<P>you can find used parts in many local scrap yards. They may not be perfect but they are cheaper when you are shotgun servicing the brick. this motor was used in a numbr of GM cars thru 1992. I've seen them in Grand Am and old cutlas calais to name a couple. S10 truck also.||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||11-09-2000||12:56 PM||||Leave it to a company with resources and facilities like GM, to eliminate moving parts, and actually make an ignition <B>more</B> fussy and unreliable!<P>Knock on wood (my head - the brain box), I've had no troubles yet... ||||reg||9
Z||000006||theogre||11-09-2000||01:48 PM||||when you figure the thing is 12-13 years old and has likely never been serviced at all..... How many distributors can you say that about?<P>Don't be giping about the hardware if you didn't do your homework first. There's a whole bunch of information available for servicing DIS ignitions.<P>197$ for a PIM? Pep boys and most of the other large chains have them for about 120-130$us. Carparts.com wrenchead etc all have them too. Along with the coils and crank sensor.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||11-09-2000||02:39 PM||||Well, I can think of two...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Point taken.  When I said "fussy," think I was referring to Ogre's comment that the DIS tends to be more picky about proper spark plug gap.  And in fairness, my own 1988's DIS is still going strong   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">       <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 11-09-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000008||DaveL||11-09-2000||03:55 PM||||I had  exactly the same problems with my 87 SE 2.5 DIS . I replaced the ignition module and the crank sensor, no good. Then replaced the ECM and it was off to the races I went!<BR>Good Luck!||||reg||10
Z||000009||theogre||11-09-2000||07:51 PM||||Often just reseating the connectors can help these ECMs. They use solder tinned "edge connection," and the solder can build up oxide that skews sensor inputs.<P>You can get used ECM's out of Grand Am, Cutlas Calais, and other GM cars with this motor. You can also get them from other cars with DIS built between 87 and 92. You want the ones labled 1227748<P>What you can't do is use the EPROM from those cars in Fiero. You must use a Fiero EPROM or you'll have problems, even if it was the same motor in another car. <P>Most other cars with this motor use a diferant type VSS. The ECM's VSS input channel is selected by the EPROM. (There are 2 differant VSS inputs on the 1227748 ECM.) So for exapmle if you take a EPROM from a 88 Grand Am and stick it in Fiero you'll get error 24 dead VSS. If you connect an ECM scaner to the car you will read 0 mph at all times. Put the Fiero EPROM back and whala the VSS works again.<P>From <A HREF="http://www.cruzers.com/~ludis/" TARGET=_blank>Ludis' GMECM site</A>:<P>Application: <BR>1987-91 2.0 OHC L4 TBI “K” LT2<BR>1990-91 2.2 L4 TBI “G” LM3<BR>1987-92 2.5 L4 TBI “R” LR8 (exc. “W” body)<BR>1987-91 2.5 L4 TBI “U” L68 <P>(year, size, type, efi, vin letter, rpo)<P>Motor age has a very good series of three articles on DIS. <A HREF="http://www.motorage.com" TARGET=_blank>www.motorage.com</A>  search for GM DIS. Note, part 2 of the series is named diferantly but is in the search list. Part 2 covers 2.5l DIS specifics.<P>ACdelco now has a bunch of little tech blurbs about differant things. <A HREF="http://www.acdelco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.acdelco.com</A>  there is a good diagram of DIS showing the notch wheel.||||reg||1
